{"timestamp":"2026-09-05T01:20:35.549Z","ordinal":19976,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a06cdc-629f-7b82-8a80-b940922d7571","turn_id":"01a06cdc-6399-7b30-9fa8-4b87f0cfba0e","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-69f2afec-979a-4d4a-b396-c8c7b4dee312","process_id":"41630","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat src/server/AGENTS.md && rg --files src/server/effect src/db -g AGENTS.md && cat src/db/AGENTS.md && sed -n '1,150p' repos/effect/LLMS.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/hedde/.codex/worktrees/aa9e/evorto","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat src/server/AGENTS.md","name":"AGENTS.md","path":"src/server/AGENTS.md"},{"type":"list_files","cmd":"rg --files src/server/effect src/db -g AGENTS.md","path":"effect"},{"type":"read","cmd":"cat src/db/AGENTS.md","name":"AGENTS.md","path":"src/db/AGENTS.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,150p' repos/effect/LLMS.md","name":"LLMS.md","path":"repos/effect/LLMS.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Server Guidelines\n\n## Runtime Architecture\n\n- Prefer Effect and Effect Platform first.\n- For Effect v4 reference material, read `repos/effect/LLMS.md` first, then inspect the vendored Effect source under `repos/effect/packages/**` for implementation details, tests, and examples.\n- Treat `repos/effect` as read-only reference material. Do not import from it; app code should keep importing from normal Effect packages.\n- Organize server capabilities with Effect dependency injection (`Context.Service` + composed `Layer`s).\n- Keep service dependencies declared in service definitions; wire app composition with flat `Layer.mergeAll` / `Layer.provideMerge`.\n- Keep runtime configuration centralized in `src/server/config/**`; prefer native Effect `Config.*` combinators and resolve optional/default behavior at that boundary.\n- Preserve honest config types at the config boundary; prefer `Option` for meaningful absence and only flatten to `undefined` or plain values at the consumer boundary that actually needs it.\n- Use Bun-native capabilities when Effect does not provide the needed primitive.\n- Do not introduce new Express/Hono server paths.\n\n## API and Validation\n\n- Keep API contracts in Effect RPC + Effect `Schema`.\n- Server boundaries must use Effect `Schema` for validated input/output.\n- Maintain fully typed input/output boundaries and explicit error mapping.\n- Reuse shared RPC contracts from `src/shared/rpc-contracts/**`.\n\n## Auth, Storage, and Integrations\n\n- Keep Auth0 integration aligned with `@auth0/auth0-server-js` architecture.\n- For Auth0 SDK calls, keep `Effect.tryPromise(...)` and handle only known SDK/domain errors explicitly.\n- Do not convert unknown Auth0/runtime failures into `undefined`/silent fallback values.\n- For known Auth0 errors, map to deterministic HTTP/domain responses; for unknown errors, fail and log with context.\n- Reference known Auth0 error classes from upstream when defining mappings: [auth0-server-js errors.ts](https://github.com/auth0/auth0-auth-js/blob/main/packages/auth0-server-js/src/errors.ts).\n- Prefer Bun-native storage clients (for example Bun `S3Client`) for object storage integrations.\n- Keep server-side security headers and webhook protections aligned with current runtime middleware.\n- Event registration and add-on charges are Stripe-only. Reject paid event\n  configuration or execution without the tenant's connected Stripe account;\n  do not add a cash/manual paid-event fallback.\n- Treat registration transfer as one inseparable registration/add-on bundle.\n  Preserve guest quantity, every included/free/purchased add-on quantity, and\n  check-in/fulfillment history. Calculate recipient pricing independently at\n  current base prices with recipient-current discounts only, refund each\n  original Stripe source exactly, and use database-only completion only when\n  the whole bundle is free and no refund is required.\n- Google Maps is required production functionality. Cloudflare Images is being\n  removed and must not gain new product coupling or release-gate requirements.\n- Dispatch each queued email once. Keep explicit provider rejections and\n  delivery-unknown outcomes stored and read-only; never requeue or resend an\n  ambiguous outcome automatically.\n\n## Logging\n\n- Use Effect platform logging (`Effect.log`, `Effect.logInfo`, `Effect.logWarning`, `Effect.logError`).\n- Prefer structured log annotations (`Effect.annotateLogs`) over interpolated log strings.\n- Do not use `consola` or direct `console.*` in server runtime code.\n- After every server file edit, run `bun run lint` and `bun run format:write`.\n- Before calling WebStorm `get_file_problems` on edited server files, run `bun run lint` first.\n- Markdown files do not need a WebStorm `get_file_problems` pass.\n- After editing a server file, run WebStorm `get_file_problems` on that file when possible before finishing.\nsrc/db/AGENTS.md\nsrc/server/effect/AGENTS.md\n# Database Guidelines\n\n- Drizzle schema is the source of truth for persisted shapes.\n- For Drizzle reference material, inspect the vendored upstream source under `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/**` first.\n- For relation behavior, query builders, dialect details, and type inference, prefer upstream tests in `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/tests/**`, `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/type-tests/**`, and `repos/drizzle/integration-tests/**` over stale examples.\n- Treat `repos/drizzle` as read-only reference material. Do not import from it; app code should keep importing from normal Drizzle packages.\n- Prefer inferred Drizzle types across callers; avoid duplicate handwritten DB model types.\n- Apply local schema changes from the current Drizzle source with\n  `bun run db:push` or `bun run db:reset`.\n- Hosted schema changes are explained and allowlisted before the private `ops`\n  role applies the packaged Drizzle schema. Do not add a parallel repo-local\n  migration or compatibility path.\n- Avoid `any`/unchecked casts in query helpers.\n- Server-side Effect authorization is authoritative; no PostgreSQL RLS layer is\n  planned.\n- Composite tenant foreign keys, `NO ACTION` relationships, and database\n  constraints are integrity safeguards, not authorization mechanisms.\n- When changing schema or constraints, document required local reset/setup steps in track/handoff docs.\n- After every DB file edit, run `bun run lint` and `bun run format:write`.\n- Before calling WebStorm `get_file_problems` on edited DB files, run `bun run lint` first.\n- Markdown files do not need a WebStorm `get_file_problems` pass.\n- After editing a DB file, run WebStorm `get_file_problems` on that file when possible before finishing.\n\n## Drizzle Relations v2 Schema Design Notes\n\n- Define Drizzle relations for query ergonomics, but still define real database foreign keys and constraints in schema tables; relations alone do not create DB constraints.\n- For many-to-many, model an explicit junction table and prefer a composite primary key on the pair of foreign keys.\n- If using `through(...)` for relation traversal, index each foreign key column and add a composite index on the pair to support both directions and relation resolution efficiently.\n- Use relation `alias` when two tables connect in multiple ways (for example `author` and `reviewer`) to avoid ambiguity.\n- Only mark a relation as non-optional when the linked row is guaranteed to exist; keep nullable/optional relation types accurate.\n- If relation definitions are split with `defineRelationsPart`, merge parts without overwriting table entries and keep one base part that includes all tables for complete type inference.\n- Predefined relation `where` filters operate on the target (`to`) table; design polymorphic relation filters with that scope in mind.\n# Effect library documentation\n\nThis documentation resides in the Effect monorepo, which contains the source\ncode for the Effect library and its related packages.\n\nWhen you need to find any information about the Effect library, only use this\ndocumentation and the source code found in `./packages`. Do not use\n`node_modules` or any other external documentation, as it may be outdated or\nincorrect.\n\n**Note**: The examples in this documentation contain comments for illustration\npurposes. In practice, you would not include these comments in your code.\n\n## Writing `Effect` code\n\nPrefer writing Effect code with `Effect.gen` & `Effect.fn(\"name\")`. Then attach\nadditional behaviour with combinators. This style is more readable and easier to\nmaintain than using combinators alone.\n\n### Using Effect.gen\n\nUse `Effect.gen` to write code in an imperative style similar to async await.\nYou can use `yield*` to access the result of an effect.\n\n```ts\nimport { Effect, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\nEffect.gen(function*() {\n  yield* Effect.log(\"Starting the file processing...\")\n  yield* Effect.log(\"Reading file...\")\n\n  // Always return when raising an error, to ensure typescript understands that\n  // the function will not continue executing.\n  return yield* new FileProcessingError({ message: \"Failed to read the file\" })\n}).pipe(\n  // Add additional functionality with .pipe\n  Effect.catch((error) => Effect.logError(`An error occurred: ${error}`)),\n  Effect.withSpan(\"fileProcessing\", {\n    attributes: {\n      method: \"Effect.gen\"\n    }\n  })\n)\n\n// Use Schema.TaggedErrorClass to define a custom error\nexport class FileProcessingError extends Schema.TaggedErrorClass<FileProcessingError>()(\"FileProcessingError\", {\n  message: Schema.String\n}) {}\n```\n\n### Using Effect.fn\n\nWhen writing functions that return an Effect, use `Effect.fn` to use the\ngenerator syntax.\n\n**Avoid creating functions that return an Effect.gen**, use `Effect.fn`\ninstead.\n\n```ts\nimport { Effect, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\n// Pass a string to Effect.fn, which will improve stack traces and also\n// attach a tracing span (using Effect.withSpan behind the scenes).\n//\n// The name string should match the function name.\n//\nexport const effectFunction = Effect.fn(\"effectFunction\")(\n  // You can use `Effect.fn.Return` to specify the return type of the function.\n  // It accepts the same type parameters as `Effect.Effect`.\n  function*(n: number): Effect.fn.Return<string, SomeError> {\n    yield* Effect.logInfo(\"Received number:\", n)\n\n    // Always return when raising an error, to ensure typescript understands that\n    // the function will not continue executing.\n    return yield* new SomeError({ message: \"Failed to read the file\" })\n  },\n  // Add additional functionality by passing in additional arguments.\n  // **Do not** use .pipe with Effect.fn\n  Effect.catch((error) => Effect.logError(`An error occurred: ${error}`)),\n  Effect.annotateLogs({\n    method: \"effectFunction\"\n  })\n)\n\n// Use Schema.TaggedErrorClass to define a custom error\nexport class SomeError extends Schema.TaggedErrorClass<SomeError>()(\"SomeError\", {\n  message: Schema.String\n}) {}\n```\n\n### More examples\n\n- **[Creating effects from common sources](./ai-docs/src/01_effect/01_basics/10_creating-effects.ts)**:\n  Learn how to create effects from various sources, including plain values,\n  synchronous code, Promise APIs, optional values, and callback-based APIs.\n\n## Defining schemas and domain models\n\nAll validation and domain modeling in Effect is done with `Schema`.\n\n**AVOID using predicates or manual parsing**, instead use `Schema` to parse untrusted data and validate it.\n\nFor a comprehensive guide, see [packages/effect/SCHEMA.md](./packages/effect/SCHEMA.md). Make sure to read the guide in chunks, as it is a large document.\n\n- **[Schema basics](./ai-docs/src/01_effect/02_schema/10_schema-basics.ts)**:\n  Define `Schema.Class`s, decode unknown input into typed values, and\n  encode typed values back into their external representation.\n\n## Writing Effect services\n\nEffect services are the most common way to structure Effect code. Prefer using\nservices to encapsulate behaviour over other approaches, as it ensures that your\ncode is modular, testable, and maintainable.\n\n### Context.Service\n\nThe default way to define a service is to extend `Context.Service`,\npassing in the service interface as a type parameter.\n\n```ts\n// file: src/db/Database.ts\nimport { Context, Effect, Layer, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\n// Pass in the service class name as the first type parameter, and the service\n// interface as the second type parameter.\nexport class Database extends Context.Service<Database, {\n  query(sql: string): Effect.Effect<Array<unknown>, DatabaseError>\n}>()(\n  // The string identifier for the service, which should include the package\n  // name and the subdirectory path to the service file.\n  \"myapp/db/Database\"\n) {\n  // Attach a static layer to the service, which will be used to provide an\n  // implementation of the service.\n  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(\n    Database,\n    Effect.gen(function*() {\n      // Define the service methods using Effect.fn\n      const query = Effect.fn(\"Database.query\")(function*(sql: string) {\n        yield* Effect.log(\"Executing SQL query:\", sql)\n        return [{ id: 1, name: \"Alice\" }, { id: 2, name: \"Bob\" }]\n      })\n\n      // Return an instance of the service using Database.of, passing in an\n      // object that implements the service interface.\n      return Database.of({\n        query\n      })\n    })\n  )\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Server Guidelines\n\n## Runtime Architecture\n\n- Prefer Effect and Effect Platform first.\n- For Effect v4 reference material, read `repos/effect/LLMS.md` first, then inspect the vendored Effect source under `repos/effect/packages/**` for implementation details, tests, and examples.\n- Treat `repos/effect` as read-only reference material. Do not import from it; app code should keep importing from normal Effect packages.\n- Organize server capabilities with Effect dependency injection (`Context.Service` + composed `Layer`s).\n- Keep service dependencies declared in service definitions; wire app composition with flat `Layer.mergeAll` / `Layer.provideMerge`.\n- Keep runtime configuration centralized in `src/server/config/**`; prefer native Effect `Config.*` combinators and resolve optional/default behavior at that boundary.\n- Preserve honest config types at the config boundary; prefer `Option` for meaningful absence and only flatten to `undefined` or plain values at the consumer boundary that actually needs it.\n- Use Bun-native capabilities when Effect does not provide the needed primitive.\n- Do not introduce new Express/Hono server paths.\n\n## API and Validation\n\n- Keep API contracts in Effect RPC + Effect `Schema`.\n- Server boundaries must use Effect `Schema` for validated input/output.\n- Maintain fully typed input/output boundaries and explicit error mapping.\n- Reuse shared RPC contracts from `src/shared/rpc-contracts/**`.\n\n## Auth, Storage, and Integrations\n\n- Keep Auth0 integration aligned with `@auth0/auth0-server-js` architecture.\n- For Auth0 SDK calls, keep `Effect.tryPromise(...)` and handle only known SDK/domain errors explicitly.\n- Do not convert unknown Auth0/runtime failures into `undefined`/silent fallback values.\n- For known Auth0 errors, map to deterministic HTTP/domain responses; for unknown errors, fail and log with context.\n- Reference known Auth0 error classes from upstream when defining mappings: [auth0-server-js errors.ts](https://github.com/auth0/auth0-auth-js/blob/main/packages/auth0-server-js/src/errors.ts).\n- Prefer Bun-native storage clients (for example Bun `S3Client`) for object storage integrations.\n- Keep server-side security headers and webhook protections aligned with current runtime middleware.\n- Event registration and add-on charges are Stripe-only. Reject paid event\n  configuration or execution without the tenant's connected Stripe account;\n  do not add a cash/manual paid-event fallback.\n- Treat registration transfer as one inseparable registration/add-on bundle.\n  Preserve guest quantity, every included/free/purchased add-on quantity, and\n  check-in/fulfillment history. Calculate recipient pricing independently at\n  current base prices with recipient-current discounts only, refund each\n  original Stripe source exactly, and use database-only completion only when\n  the whole bundle is free and no refund is required.\n- Google Maps is required production functionality. Cloudflare Images is being\n  removed and must not gain new product coupling or release-gate requirements.\n- Dispatch each queued email once. Keep explicit provider rejections and\n  delivery-unknown outcomes stored and read-only; never requeue or resend an\n  ambiguous outcome automatically.\n\n## Logging\n\n- Use Effect platform logging (`Effect.log`, `Effect.logInfo`, `Effect.logWarning`, `Effect.logError`).\n- Prefer structured log annotations (`Effect.annotateLogs`) over interpolated log strings.\n- Do not use `consola` or direct `console.*` in server runtime code.\n- After every server file edit, run `bun run lint` and `bun run format:write`.\n- Before calling WebStorm `get_file_problems` on edited server files, run `bun run lint` first.\n- Markdown files do not need a WebStorm `get_file_problems` pass.\n- After editing a server file, run WebStorm `get_file_problems` on that file when possible before finishing.\nsrc/db/AGENTS.md\nsrc/server/effect/AGENTS.md\n# Database Guidelines\n\n- Drizzle schema is the source of truth for persisted shapes.\n- For Drizzle reference material, inspect the vendored upstream source under `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/**` first.\n- For relation behavior, query builders, dialect details, and type inference, prefer upstream tests in `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/tests/**`, `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/type-tests/**`, and `repos/drizzle/integration-tests/**` over stale examples.\n- Treat `repos/drizzle` as read-only reference material. Do not import from it; app code should keep importing from normal Drizzle packages.\n- Prefer inferred Drizzle types across callers; avoid duplicate handwritten DB model types.\n- Apply local schema changes from the current Drizzle source with\n  `bun run db:push` or `bun run db:reset`.\n- Hosted schema changes are explained and allowlisted before the private `ops`\n  role applies the packaged Drizzle schema. Do not add a parallel repo-local\n  migration or compatibility path.\n- Avoid `any`/unchecked casts in query helpers.\n- Server-side Effect authorization is authoritative; no PostgreSQL RLS layer is\n  planned.\n- Composite tenant foreign keys, `NO ACTION` relationships, and database\n  constraints are integrity safeguards, not authorization mechanisms.\n- When changing schema or constraints, document required local reset/setup steps in track/handoff docs.\n- After every DB file edit, run `bun run lint` and `bun run format:write`.\n- Before calling WebStorm `get_file_problems` on edited DB files, run `bun run lint` first.\n- Markdown files do not need a WebStorm `get_file_problems` pass.\n- After editing a DB file, run WebStorm `get_file_problems` on that file when possible before finishing.\n\n## Drizzle Relations v2 Schema Design Notes\n\n- Define Drizzle relations for query ergonomics, but still define real database foreign keys and constraints in schema tables; relations alone do not create DB constraints.\n- For many-to-many, model an explicit junction table and prefer a composite primary key on the pair of foreign keys.\n- If using `through(...)` for relation traversal, index each foreign key column and add a composite index on the pair to support both directions and relation resolution efficiently.\n- Use relation `alias` when two tables connect in multiple ways (for example `author` and `reviewer`) to avoid ambiguity.\n- Only mark a relation as non-optional when the linked row is guaranteed to exist; keep nullable/optional relation types accurate.\n- If relation definitions are split with `defineRelationsPart`, merge parts without overwriting table entries and keep one base part that includes all tables for complete type inference.\n- Predefined relation `where` filters operate on the target (`to`) table; design polymorphic relation filters with that scope in mind.\n# Effect library documentation\n\nThis documentation resides in the Effect monorepo, which contains the source\ncode for the Effect library and its related packages.\n\nWhen you need to find any information about the Effect library, only use this\ndocumentation and the source code found in `./packages`. Do not use\n`node_modules` or any other external documentation, as it may be outdated or\nincorrect.\n\n**Note**: The examples in this documentation contain comments for illustration\npurposes. In practice, you would not include these comments in your code.\n\n## Writing `Effect` code\n\nPrefer writing Effect code with `Effect.gen` & `Effect.fn(\"name\")`. Then attach\nadditional behaviour with combinators. This style is more readable and easier to\nmaintain than using combinators alone.\n\n### Using Effect.gen\n\nUse `Effect.gen` to write code in an imperative style similar to async await.\nYou can use `yield*` to access the result of an effect.\n\n```ts\nimport { Effect, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\nEffect.gen(function*() {\n  yield* Effect.log(\"Starting the file processing...\")\n  yield* Effect.log(\"Reading file...\")\n\n  // Always return when raising an error, to ensure typescript understands that\n  // the function will not continue executing.\n  return yield* new FileProcessingError({ message: \"Failed to read the file\" })\n}).pipe(\n  // Add additional functionality with .pipe\n  Effect.catch((error) => Effect.logError(`An error occurred: ${error}`)),\n  Effect.withSpan(\"fileProcessing\", {\n    attributes: {\n      method: \"Effect.gen\"\n    }\n  })\n)\n\n// Use Schema.TaggedErrorClass to define a custom error\nexport class FileProcessingError extends Schema.TaggedErrorClass<FileProcessingError>()(\"FileProcessingError\", {\n  message: Schema.String\n}) {}\n```\n\n### Using Effect.fn\n\nWhen writing functions that return an Effect, use `Effect.fn` to use the\ngenerator syntax.\n\n**Avoid creating functions that return an Effect.gen**, use `Effect.fn`\ninstead.\n\n```ts\nimport { Effect, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\n// Pass a string to Effect.fn, which will improve stack traces and also\n// attach a tracing span (using Effect.withSpan behind the scenes).\n//\n// The name string should match the function name.\n//\nexport const effectFunction = Effect.fn(\"effectFunction\")(\n  // You can use `Effect.fn.Return` to specify the return type of the function.\n  // It accepts the same type parameters as `Effect.Effect`.\n  function*(n: number): Effect.fn.Return<string, SomeError> {\n    yield* Effect.logInfo(\"Received number:\", n)\n\n    // Always return when raising an error, to ensure typescript understands that\n    // the function will not continue executing.\n    return yield* new SomeError({ message: \"Failed to read the file\" })\n  },\n  // Add additional functionality by passing in additional arguments.\n  // **Do not** use .pipe with Effect.fn\n  Effect.catch((error) => Effect.logError(`An error occurred: ${error}`)),\n  Effect.annotateLogs({\n    method: \"effectFunction\"\n  })\n)\n\n// Use Schema.TaggedErrorClass to define a custom error\nexport class SomeError extends Schema.TaggedErrorClass<SomeError>()(\"SomeError\", {\n  message: Schema.String\n}) {}\n```\n\n### More examples\n\n- **[Creating effects from common sources](./ai-docs/src/01_effect/01_basics/10_creating-effects.ts)**:\n  Learn how to create effects from various sources, including plain values,\n  synchronous code, Promise APIs, optional values, and callback-based APIs.\n\n## Defining schemas and domain models\n\nAll validation and domain modeling in Effect is done with `Schema`.\n\n**AVOID using predicates or manual parsing**, instead use `Schema` to parse untrusted data and validate it.\n\nFor a comprehensive guide, see [packages/effect/SCHEMA.md](./packages/effect/SCHEMA.md). Make sure to read the guide in chunks, as it is a large document.\n\n- **[Schema basics](./ai-docs/src/01_effect/02_schema/10_schema-basics.ts)**:\n  Define `Schema.Class`s, decode unknown input into typed values, and\n  encode typed values back into their external representation.\n\n## Writing Effect services\n\nEffect services are the most common way to structure Effect code. Prefer using\nservices to encapsulate behaviour over other approaches, as it ensures that your\ncode is modular, testable, and maintainable.\n\n### Context.Service\n\nThe default way to define a service is to extend `Context.Service`,\npassing in the service interface as a type parameter.\n\n```ts\n// file: src/db/Database.ts\nimport { Context, Effect, Layer, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\n// Pass in the service class name as the first type parameter, and the service\n// interface as the second type parameter.\nexport class Database extends Context.Service<Database, {\n  query(sql: string): Effect.Effect<Array<unknown>, DatabaseError>\n}>()(\n  // The string identifier for the service, which should include the package\n  // name and the subdirectory path to the service file.\n  \"myapp/db/Database\"\n) {\n  // Attach a static layer to the service, which will be used to provide an\n  // implementation of the service.\n  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(\n    Database,\n    Effect.gen(function*() {\n      // Define the service methods using Effect.fn\n      const query = Effect.fn(\"Database.query\")(function*(sql: string) {\n        yield* Effect.log(\"Executing SQL query:\", sql)\n        return [{ id: 1, name: \"Alice\" }, { id: 2, name: \"Bob\" }]\n      })\n\n      // Return an instance of the service using Database.of, passing in an\n      // object that implements the service interface.\n      return Database.of({\n        query\n      })\n    })\n  )\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":4541},"formatted_output":"# Server Guidelines\n\n## Runtime Architecture\n\n- Prefer Effect and Effect Platform first.\n- For Effect v4 reference material, read `repos/effect/LLMS.md` first, then inspect the vendored Effect source under `repos/effect/packages/**` for implementation details, tests, and examples.\n- Treat `repos/effect` as read-only reference material. Do not import from it; app code should keep importing from normal Effect packages.\n- Organize server capabilities with Effect dependency injection (`Context.Service` + composed `Layer`s).\n- Keep service dependencies declared in service definitions; wire app composition with flat `Layer.mergeAll` / `Layer.provideMerge`.\n- Keep runtime configuration centralized in `src/server/config/**`; prefer native Effect `Config.*` combinators and resolve optional/default behavior at that boundary.\n- Preserve honest config types at the config boundary; prefer `Option` for meaningful absence and only flatten to `undefined` or plain values at the consumer boundary that actually needs it.\n- Use Bun-native capabilities when Effect does not provide the needed primitive.\n- Do not introduce new Express/Hono server paths.\n\n## API and Validation\n\n- Keep API contracts in Effect RPC + Effect `Schema`.\n- Server boundaries must use Effect `Schema` for validated input/output.\n- Maintain fully typed input/output boundaries and explicit error mapping.\n- Reuse shared RPC contracts from `src/shared/rpc-contracts/**`.\n\n## Auth, Storage, and Integrations\n\n- Keep Auth0 integration aligned with `@auth0/auth0-server-js` architecture.\n- For Auth0 SDK calls, keep `Effect.tryPromise(...)` and handle only known SDK/domain errors explicitly.\n- Do not convert unknown Auth0/runtime failures into `undefined`/silent fallback values.\n- For known Auth0 errors, map to deterministic HTTP/domain responses; for unknown errors, fail and log with context.\n- Reference known Auth0 error classes from upstream when defining mappings: [auth0-server-js errors.ts](https://github.com/auth0/auth0-auth-js/blob/main/packages/auth0-server-js/src/errors.ts).\n- Prefer Bun-native storage clients (for example Bun `S3Client`) for object storage integrations.\n- Keep server-side security headers and webhook protections aligned with current runtime middleware.\n- Event registration and add-on charges are Stripe-only. Reject paid event\n  configuration or execution without the tenant's connected Stripe account;\n  do not add a cash/manual paid-event fallback.\n- Treat registration transfer as one inseparable registration/add-on bundle.\n  Preserve guest quantity, every included/free/purchased add-on quantity, and\n  check-in/fulfillment history. Calculate recipient pricing independently at\n  current base prices with recipient-current discounts only, refund each\n  original Stripe source exactly, and use database-only completion only when\n  the whole bundle is free and no refund is required.\n- Google Maps is required production functionality. Cloudflare Images is being\n  removed and must not gain new product coupling or release-gate requirements.\n- Dispatch each queued email once. Keep explicit provider rejections and\n  delivery-unknown outcomes stored and read-only; never requeue or resend an\n  ambiguous outcome automatically.\n\n## Logging\n\n- Use Effect platform logging (`Effect.log`, `Effect.logInfo`, `Effect.logWarning`, `Effect.logError`).\n- Prefer structured log annotations (`Effect.annotateLogs`) over interpolated log strings.\n- Do not use `consola` or direct `console.*` in server runtime code.\n- After every server file edit, run `bun run lint` and `bun run format:write`.\n- Before calling WebStorm `get_file_problems` on edited server files, run `bun run lint` first.\n- Markdown files do not need a WebStorm `get_file_problems` pass.\n- After editing a server file, run WebStorm `get_file_problems` on that file when possible before finishing.\nsrc/db/AGENTS.md\nsrc/server/effect/AGENTS.md\n# Database Guidelines\n\n- Drizzle schema is the source of truth for persisted shapes.\n- For Drizzle reference material, inspect the vendored upstream source under `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/**` first.\n- For relation behavior, query builders, dialect details, and type inference, prefer upstream tests in `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/tests/**`, `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/type-tests/**`, and `repos/drizzle/integration-tests/**` over stale examples.\n- Treat `repos/drizzle` as read-only reference material. Do not import from it; app code should keep importing from normal Drizzle packages.\n- Prefer inferred Drizzle types across callers; avoid duplicate handwritten DB model types.\n- Apply local schema changes from the current Drizzle source with\n  `bun run db:push` or `bun run db:reset`.\n- Hosted schema changes are explained and allowlisted before the private `ops`\n  role applies the packaged Drizzle schema. Do not add a parallel repo-local\n  migration or compatibility path.\n- Avoid `any`/unchecked casts in query helpers.\n- Server-side Effect authorization is authoritative; no PostgreSQL RLS layer is\n  planned.\n- Composite tenant foreign keys, `NO ACTION` relationships, and database\n  constraints are integrity safeguards, not authorization mechanisms.\n- When changing schema or constraints, document required local reset/setup steps in track/handoff docs.\n- After every DB file edit, run `bun run lint` and `bun run format:write`.\n- Before calling WebStorm `get_file_problems` on edited DB files, run `bun run lint` first.\n- Markdown files do not need a WebStorm `get_file_problems` pass.\n- After editing a DB file, run WebStorm `get_file_problems` on that file when possible before finishing.\n\n## Drizzle Relations v2 Schema Design Notes\n\n- Define Drizzle relations for query ergonomics, but still define real database foreign keys and constraints in schema tables; relations alone do not create DB constraints.\n- For many-to-many, model an explicit junction table and prefer a composite primary key on the pair of foreign keys.\n- If using `through(...)` for relation traversal, index each foreign key column and add a composite index on the pair to support both directions and relation resolution efficiently.\n- Use relation `alias` when two tables connect in multiple ways (for example `author` and `reviewer`) to avoid ambiguity.\n- Only mark a relation as non-optional when the linked row is guaranteed to exist; keep nullable/optional relation types accurate.\n- If relation definitions are split with `defineRelationsPart`, merge parts without overwriting table entries and keep one base part that includes all tables for complete type inference.\n- Predefined relation `where` filters operate on the target (`to`) table; design polymorphic relation filters with that scope in mind.\n# Effect library documentation\n\nThis documentation resides in the Effect monorepo, which contains the source\ncode for the Effect library and its related packages.\n\nWhen you need to find any information about the Effect library, only use this\ndocumentation and the source code found in `./packages`. Do not use\n`node_modules` or any other external documentation, as it may be outdated or\nincorrect.\n\n**Note**: The examples in this documentation contain comments for illustration\npurposes. In practice, you would not include these comments in your code.\n\n## Writing `Effect` code\n\nPrefer writing Effect code with `Effect.gen` & `Effect.fn(\"name\")`. Then attach\nadditional behaviour with combinators. This style is more readable and easier to\nmaintain than using combinators alone.\n\n### Using Effect.gen\n\nUse `Effect.gen` to write code in an imperative style similar to async await.\nYou can use `yield*` to access the result of an effect.\n\n```ts\nimport { Effect, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\nEffect.gen(function*() {\n  yield* Effect.log(\"Starting the file processing...\")\n  yield* Effect.log(\"Reading file...\")\n\n  // Always return when raising an error, to ensure typescript understands that\n  // the function will not continue executing.\n  return yield* new FileProcessingError({ message: \"Failed to read the file\" })\n}).pipe(\n  // Add additional functionality with .pipe\n  Effect.catch((error) => Effect.logError(`An error occurred: ${error}`)),\n  Effect.withSpan(\"fileProcessing\", {\n    attributes: {\n      method: \"Effect.gen\"\n    }\n  })\n)\n\n// Use Schema.TaggedErrorClass to define a custom error\nexport class FileProcessingError extends Schema.TaggedErrorClass<FileProcessingError>()(\"FileProcessingError\", {\n  message: Schema.String\n}) {}\n```\n\n### Using Effect.fn\n\nWhen writing functions that return an Effect, use `Effect.fn` to use the\ngenerator syntax.\n\n**Avoid creating functions that return an Effect.gen**, use `Effect.fn`\ninstead.\n\n```ts\nimport { Effect, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\n// Pass a string to Effect.fn, which will improve stack traces and also\n// attach a tracing span (using Effect.withSpan behind the scenes).\n//\n// The name string should match the function name.\n//\nexport const effectFunction = Effect.fn(\"effectFunction\")(\n  // You can use `Effect.fn.Return` to specify the return type of the function.\n  // It accepts the same type parameters as `Effect.Effect`.\n  function*(n: number): Effect.fn.Return<string, SomeError> {\n    yield* Effect.logInfo(\"Received number:\", n)\n\n    // Always return when raising an error, to ensure typescript understands that\n    // the function will not continue executing.\n    return yield* new SomeError({ message: \"Failed to read the file\" })\n  },\n  // Add additional functionality by passing in additional arguments.\n  // **Do not** use .pipe with Effect.fn\n  Effect.catch((error) => Effect.logError(`An error occurred: ${error}`)),\n  Effect.annotateLogs({\n    method: \"effectFunction\"\n  })\n)\n\n// Use Schema.TaggedErrorClass to define a custom error\nexport class SomeError extends Schema.TaggedErrorClass<SomeError>()(\"SomeError\", {\n  message: Schema.String\n}) {}\n```\n\n### More examples\n\n- **[Creating effects from common sources](./ai-docs/src/01_effect/01_basics/10_creating-effects.ts)**:\n  Learn how to create effects from various sources, including plain values,\n  synchronous code, Promise APIs, optional values, and callback-based APIs.\n\n## Defining schemas and domain models\n\nAll validation and domain modeling in Effect is done with `Schema`.\n\n**AVOID using predicates or manual parsing**, instead use `Schema` to parse untrusted data and validate it.\n\nFor a comprehensive guide, see [packages/effect/SCHEMA.md](./packages/effect/SCHEMA.md). Make sure to read the guide in chunks, as it is a large document.\n\n- **[Schema basics](./ai-docs/src/01_effect/02_schema/10_schema-basics.ts)**:\n  Define `Schema.Class`s, decode unknown input into typed values, and\n  encode typed values back into their external representation.\n\n## Writing Effect services\n\nEffect services are the most common way to structure Effect code. Prefer using\nservices to encapsulate behaviour over other approaches, as it ensures that your\ncode is modular, testable, and maintainable.\n\n### Context.Service\n\nThe default way to define a service is to extend `Context.Service`,\npassing in the service interface as a type parameter.\n\n```ts\n// file: src/db/Database.ts\nimport { Context, Effect, Layer, Schema } from \"effect\"\n\n// Pass in the service class name as the first type parameter, and the service\n// interface as the second type parameter.\nexport class Database extends Context.Service<Database, {\n  query(sql: string): Effect.Effect<Array<unknown>, DatabaseError>\n}>()(\n  // The string identifier for the service, which should include the package\n  // name and the subdirectory path to the service file.\n  \"myapp/db/Database\"\n) {\n  // Attach a static layer to the service, which will be used to provide an\n  // implementation of the service.\n  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(\n    Database,\n    Effect.gen(function*() {\n      // Define the service methods using Effect.fn\n      const query = Effect.fn(\"Database.query\")(function*(sql: string) {\n        yield* Effect.log(\"Executing SQL query:\", sql)\n        return [{ id: 1, name: \"Alice\" }, { id: 2, name: \"Bob\" }]\n      })\n\n      // Return an instance of the service using Database.of, passing in an\n      // object that implements the service interface.\n      return Database.of({\n        query\n      })\n    })\n  )\n"},"started_at_ms":1788571235549,"completed_at_ms":1788571235549}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-05T01:20:35.552Z","ordinal":19977,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a06f27-34e0-71c0-b1e9-1b377d7ae651","call_id":"call_L1pFOuFHEeIXIEfSHZn7CYc4","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"d45e62\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000004833,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":4132,\"output\":\"import { afterAll, beforeAll, describe, expect, it } from '@effect/vitest';\\nimport { and, eq, inArray } from 'drizzle-orm';\\nimport { drizzle, type NodePgDatabase } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres';\\nimport { ConfigProvider, Effect, Layer } from 'effect';\\nimport * as Headers from 'effect/unstable/http/Headers';\\nimport { Pool, type PoolClient } from 'pg';\\n\\nimport { databaseLayer } from '../../../../../db';\\nimport { createId } from '../../../../../db/create-id';\\nimport { createNodePgPoolConfig } from '../../../../../db/pg-connection-config';\\nimport { relations } from '../../../../../db/relations';\\nimport {\\n  eventInstances,\\n  eventRegistrationOptions,\\n  eventTemplateCategories,\\n  eventTemplates,\\n  platformAuditEntries,\\n  roles,\\n  rolesToTenantUsers,\\n  tenants,\\n  users,\\n  usersToTenants,\\n} from '../../../../../db/schema';\\nimport { type Permission } from '../../../../../shared/permissions/permissions';\\nimport {\\n  RpcRequestContext,\\n  type RpcRequestContextShape,\\n} from '../../../../../shared/rpc-contracts/app-rpcs';\\nimport { PlatformAdministratorAuthority } from '../../../../../types/custom/platform-authority';\\nimport { platformEventHandlers } from '../platform/platform-events.handlers';\\nimport { platformTenantAdminHandlers } from '../platform/platform-tenant-admin.handlers';\\nimport { RpcAccess } from '../shared/rpc-access.service';\\nimport { eventQueryHandlers } from './events-query.handlers';\\n\\nconst databaseUrl = process.env['DATABASE_URL'];\\nif (!databaseUrl) {\\n  throw new Error('DATABASE_URL is required for PostgreSQL integration tests');\\n}\\n\\ntype TestDatabase = NodePgDatabase<typeof relations>;\\n\\nconst tenantId = createId();\\nconst otherTenantId = createId();\\nconst creatorId = createId();\\nconst otherCreatorId = createId();\\nconst matchingUserId = createId();\\nconst nonmatchingUserId = createId();\\nconst defaultRoleId = createId();\\nconst matchingRoleId = createId();\\nconst nonmatchingRoleId = createId();\\nconst raceRoleId = createId();\\nconst otherTenantRoleId = createId();\\nconst categoryId = createId();\\nconst otherCategoryId = createId();\\nconst templateId = createId();\\nconst otherTemplateId = createId();\\nconst defaultAnnouncementId = createId();\\nconst matchingAnnouncementId = createId();\\nconst linkOnlyAnnouncementId = createId();\\nconst raceAnnouncementId = createId();\\nconst optionfulMatchingId = createId();\\nconst optionfulUnrestrictedId = createId();\\nconst optionfulDefaultRoleId = createId();\\nconst draftRestrictedId = createId();\\nconst otherTenantAnnouncementId = createId();\\nconst optionfulMatchingOptionId = createId();\\nconst optionfulUnrestrictedOptionId = createId();\\nconst optionfulDefaultRoleOptionId = createId();\\nconst draftRestrictedOptionId = createId();\\nconst membershipIds = [createId(), createId()] as const;\\nconst eventIds = [\\n  defaultAnnouncementId,\\n  matchingAnnouncementId,\\n  linkOnlyAnnouncementId,\\n  raceAnnouncementId,\\n  optionfulMatchingId,\\n  optionfulUnrestrictedId,\\n  optionfulDefaultRoleId,\\n  draftRestrictedId,\\n  otherTenantAnnouncementId,\\n] as const;\\nconst userIds = [\\n  creatorId,\\n  otherCreatorId,\\n  matchingUserId,\\n  nonmatchingUserId,\\n] as const;\\nconst handlerOptions = { headers: Headers.fromInput({}) };\\n\\nconst tenantContext = {\\n  cancellationDeadlineHoursBeforeStart: 120,\\n  currency: 'EUR' as const,\\n  defaultLocation: null,\\n  discountProviders: {\\n    esnCard: { config: {}, status: 'disabled' as const },\\n  },\\n  domain: `${tenantId}.announcement-discovery.example`,\\n  id: tenantId,\\n  maxActiveRegistrationsPerUser: 0,\\n  name: 'Announcement discovery',\\n  receiptSettings: {\\n    allowOther: false,\\n    receiptCountries: ['NL'],\\n  },\\n  refundFeesOnCancellation: true,\\n  stripeAccountId: null,\\n  theme: 'evorto' as const,\\n  timezone: 'Europe/Berlin',\\n  transferDeadlineHoursBeforeStart: 0,\\n} satisfies RpcRequestContextShape['tenant'];\\n\\nconst anonymousContext = {\\n  authData: {},\\n  authenticated: false,\\n  permissions: [],\\n  tenant: tenantContext,\\n  user: null,\\n  userAssigned: false,\\n} satisfies RpcRequestContextShape;\\n\\nconst authenticatedContext = ({\\n  permissions = [],\\n  roleIds,\\n  userId,\\n}: {\\n  permissions?: readonly Permission[];\\n  roleIds: readonly string[];\\n  userId: string;\\n}) =>\\n  ({\\n    authData: {},\\n    authenticated: true,\\n    permissions,\\n    tenant: tenantContext,\\n    user: {\\n      auth0Id: `auth0|${userId}`,\\n      communicationEmail: `${userId}@example.com`,\\n      email: `${userId}@example.com`,\\n      firstName: 'Announcement',\\n      id: userId,\\n      lastName: 'Viewer',\\n      permissions,\\n      roleIds,\\n    },\\n    userAssigned: true,\\n  }) satisfies RpcRequestContextShape;\\n\\nconst platformAuthority = PlatformAdministratorAuthority.make({\\n  actorEmail: 'platform-announcement@example.com',\\n  actorId: 'auth0|platform-announcement-discovery',\\n  kind: 'platformAdministrator',\\n});\\n\\nconst platformContext = {\\n  ...anonymousContext,\\n  authenticated: true,\\n  platformAuthority,\\n} satisfies RpcRequestContextShape;\\n\\nconst configLayer = ConfigProvider.layer(\\n  ConfigProvider.fromEnv({\\n    env: {\\n      DATABASE_TLS_REQUIRED: 'false',\\n      DATABASE_URL: databaseUrl,\\n    },\\n  }),\\n);\\nconst handlerLayer = Layer.mergeAll(\\n  databaseLayer.pipe(Layer.provide(configLayer)),\\n  RpcAccess.Default,\\n);\\n\\nconst runEventList = (\\n  context: RpcRequestContextShape,\\n  status: readonly ('APPROVED' | 'DRAFT' | 'PENDING_REVIEW')[] = ['APPROVED'],\\n) =>\\n  Effect.runPromise(\\n    eventQueryHandlers['events.eventList'](\\n      {\\n        limit: 100,\\n        offset: 0,\\n        startAfter: '2099-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\\n        status: [...status],\\n      },\\n      handlerOptions,\\n    ).pipe(\\n      Effect.provideService(RpcRequestContext, context),\\n      Effect.provide(handlerLayer),\\n    ),\\n  );\\n\\nconst listedEventIds = (\\n  days: Awaited<ReturnType<typeof runEventList>>,\\n): string[] =>\\n  days.flatMap((day) => day.events.map((event) => event.id)).toSorted();\\n\\nconst runFindOne = (context: RpcRequestContextShape, id: string) =>\\n  Effect.runPromise(\\n    eventQueryHandlers['events.findOne']({ id }, handlerOptions).pipe(\\n      Effect.provideService(RpcRequestContext, context),\\n      Effect.provide(handlerLayer),\\n    ),\\n  );\\n\\nconst runPlatformAnnouncementDiscoveryUpdate = (\\n  eventId: string,\\n  announcementRoleIds: readonly string[],\\n) =>\\n  Effect.runPromise(\\n    platformEventHandlers['platform.events.updateAnnouncementDiscovery'](\\n      {\\n        announcementRoleIds: [...announcementRoleIds],\\n        eventId,\\n        reason: 'Verify announcement discovery role integrity',\\n        targetTenantId: tenantId,\\n      },\\n      handlerOptions,\\n    ).pipe(\\n      Effect.match({\\n        onFailure: (error) => ({ error, status: 'failure' as const }),\\n        onSuccess: (event) => ({ event, status: 'success' as const }),\\n      }),\\n      Effect.provideService(RpcRequestContext, platformContext),\\n      Effect.provide(handlerLayer),\\n    ),\\n  );\\n\\nconst runRoleDeletion = () =>\\n  Effect.runPromise(\\n    platformTenantAdminHandlers['platform.roles.delete'](\\n      {\\n        reason: 'Verify announcement discovery role integrity',\\n        roleId: raceRoleId,\\n        targetTenantId: tenantId,\\n      },\\n      handlerOptions,\\n    ).pipe(\\n      Effect.match({\\n        onFailure: (error) => ({ error, status: 'failure' as const }),\\n        onSuccess: () => ({ status: 'success' as const }),\\n      }),\\n      Effect.provideService(RpcRequestContext, platformContext),\\n      Effect.provide(handlerLayer),\\n    ),\\n  );\\n\\nconst waitForBlockedRoleGraphLocks = async (\\n  pool: Pool,\\n  minimumCount: number,\\n  ensureWorkersUnsettled: () => void,\\n) => {\\n  const deadline = Date.now() + 10_000;\\n  while (Date.now() < deadline) {\\n    ensureWorkersUnsettled();\\n    const blocked = await pool.query<{ count: string }>(`\\n      SELECT count(*)::text AS count\\n      FROM pg_stat_activity\\n      WHERE datname = current_database()\\n        AND pid <> pg_backend_pid()\\n        AND wait_event_type = 'Lock'\\n        AND query ILIKE '%pg_advisory_xact_lock%'\\n    `);\\n    ensureWorkersUnsettled();\\n    if (Number(blocked.rows[0]?.count ?? 0) >= minimumCount) return;\\n    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 10));\\n  }\\n  throw new Error(\\n    `Timed out waiting for ${minimumCount} blocked role-graph locks`,\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nconst lockTenantRoleGraph = async (\\n  client: PoolClient,\\n  targetTenantId: string,\\n) => {\\n  await client.query('BEGIN');\\n  await client.query('SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended($1, 0))', [\\n    `evorto:tenant-role-graph:${targetTenantId}`,\\n  ]);\\n};\\nsrc/server/tenant-public-url-migration.postgres.spec.ts:211:  client: new Rpc.ServerClient(1),\\nsrc/server/tenant-public-url-migration.postgres.spec.ts:213:  requestId: RpcMessage.RequestId(1),\\nsrc/server/http/server-response.middleware.spec.ts:18:const makeTestHandler = Effect.fn('makeTestHandler')(function* (\\nsrc/server/http/server-response.middleware.spec.ts:138:      const { handler } = yield* makeTestHandler(\\nsrc/server/http/server-response.middleware.spec.ts:159:        const { handler } = yield* makeTestHandler(\\nsrc/server/http/server-response.middleware.spec.ts:193:      const { handler } = yield* makeTestHandler(\\nsrc/server/http/server-response.middleware.spec.ts:213:      const { handler } = yield* makeTestHandler(Layer.empty);\\nsrc/server/http/server-response.middleware.spec.ts:226:      const { handler } = yield* makeTestHandler(\\nsrc/server/http/server-response.middleware.spec.ts:243:      const { handler } = yield* makeTestHandler(\\nsrc/server/http/browser-error-telemetry.web-handler.spec.ts:5:  makeBrowserErrorTelemetryHandler,\\nsrc/server/http/browser-error-telemetry.web-handler.spec.ts:51:      const handler = makeBrowserErrorTelemetryHandler({ log, now: () => 100 });\\nsrc/server/http/browser-error-telemetry.web-handler.spec.ts:71:      const handler = makeBrowserErrorTelemetryHandler({ log, now: () => 100 });\\nsrc/server/http/browser-error-telemetry.web-handler.spec.ts:98:      const handler = makeBrowserErrorTelemetryHandler({ log, now: () => 100 });\\nsrc/server/http/browser-error-telemetry.web-handler.spec.ts:115:      const handler = makeBrowserErrorTelemetryHandler({\\nsrc/server/http/browser-error-telemetry.web-handler.spec.ts:138:      const handler = makeBrowserErrorTelemetryHandler({\\nsrc/server/auth/auth-session.spec.ts:15:  createAuth0ServerClientOptions,\\nsrc/server/auth/auth-session.spec.ts:56:  createAuth0ServerClientOptions({\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/finance/finance-receipt-concurrency.postgres.spec.ts:428:          client: new Rpc.ServerClient(1),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/finance/finance-receipt-concurrency.postgres.spec.ts:430:          requestId: RpcMessage.RequestId(1),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-query.handlers.postgres.spec.ts:88:const handlerOptions = { headers: Headers.fromInput({}) };\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-query.handlers.postgres.spec.ts:185:      handlerOptions,\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-query.handlers.postgres.spec.ts:199:    eventQueryHandlers['events.findOne']({ id }, handlerOptions).pipe(\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-query.handlers.postgres.spec.ts:217:      handlerOptions,\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-query.handlers.postgres.spec.ts:236:      handlerOptions,\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/finance/finance.handlers.spec.ts:30:  client: new Rpc.ServerClient(1),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/finance/finance.handlers.spec.ts:32:  requestId: RpcMessage.RequestId(1),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/finance/finance.handlers.spec.ts:41:  client: new Rpc.ServerClient(1),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/finance/finance.handlers.spec.ts:43:  requestId: RpcMessage.RequestId(1),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:213:const handlerOptions = <\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:229:    client: new Rpc.ServerClient(1),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:231:    requestId: RpcMessage.RequestId(1),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:1406:            handlerOptions('events.getRegistrationStatus'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:1615:            handlerOptions('events.getRegistrationStatus'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:1684:          handlerOptions('events.getRegistrationStatus'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:2098:        const attackerOptions = handlerOptions(\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4062:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4098:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4137:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4178:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4226:          handlerOptions('events.cancelEventRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4274:          handlerOptions('events.cancelEventRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4304:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistrationAddon'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4347:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4391:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4437:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4588:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4876:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4908:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4945:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:4988:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5036:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5098:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5164:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5210:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5254:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5324:        handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5355:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5389:          handlerOptions('events.cancelRegistration'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5421:        handlerOptions('events.registrationScanned'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5447:        handlerOptions('events.registrationScanned'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5482:        handlerOptions('events.registrationScanned'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5517:          handlerOptions('events.registrationScanned'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5545:          handlerOptions('events.registrationScanned'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5579:          handlerOptions('events.registrationScanned'),\\nsrc/server/effect/rpc/handlers/events/events-registration.handlers.spec.ts:5614:          handlerOptions('events.registrationScanned'),\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"87e5b9\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000004541,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":3054,\"output\":\"# Server Guidelines\\n\\n## Runtime Architecture\\n\\n- Prefer Effect and Effect Platform first.\\n- For Effect v4 reference material, read `repos/effect/LLMS.md` first, then inspect the vendored Effect source under `repos/effect/packages/**` for implementation details, tests, and examples.\\n- Treat `repos/effect` as read-only reference material. Do not import from it; app code should keep importing from normal Effect packages.\\n- Organize server capabilities with Effect dependency injection (`Context.Service` + composed `Layer`s).\\n- Keep service dependencies declared in service definitions; wire app composition with flat `Layer.mergeAll` / `Layer.provideMerge`.\\n- Keep runtime configuration centralized in `src/server/config/**`; prefer native Effect `Config.*` combinators and resolve optional/default behavior at that boundary.\\n- Preserve honest config types at the config boundary; prefer `Option` for meaningful absence and only flatten to `undefined` or plain values at the consumer boundary that actually needs it.\\n- Use Bun-native capabilities when Effect does not provide the needed primitive.\\n- Do not introduce new Express/Hono server paths.\\n\\n## API and Validation\\n\\n- Keep API contracts in Effect RPC + Effect `Schema`.\\n- Server boundaries must use Effect `Schema` for validated input/output.\\n- Maintain fully typed input/output boundaries and explicit error mapping.\\n- Reuse shared RPC contracts from `src/shared/rpc-contracts/**`.\\n\\n## Auth, Storage, and Integrations\\n\\n- Keep Auth0 integration aligned with `@auth0/auth0-server-js` architecture.\\n- For Auth0 SDK calls, keep `Effect.tryPromise(...)` and handle only known SDK/domain errors explicitly.\\n- Do not convert unknown Auth0/runtime failures into `undefined`/silent fallback values.\\n- For known Auth0 errors, map to deterministic HTTP/domain responses; for unknown errors, fail and log with context.\\n- Reference known Auth0 error classes from upstream when defining mappings: [auth0-server-js errors.ts](https://github.com/auth0/auth0-auth-js/blob/main/packages/auth0-server-js/src/errors.ts).\\n- Prefer Bun-native storage clients (for example Bun `S3Client`) for object storage integrations.\\n- Keep server-side security headers and webhook protections aligned with current runtime middleware.\\n- Event registration and add-on charges are Stripe-only. Reject paid event\\n  configuration or execution without the tenant's connected Stripe account;\\n  do not add a cash/manual paid-event fallback.\\n- Treat registration transfer as one inseparable registration/add-on bundle.\\n  Preserve guest quantity, every included/free/purchased add-on quantity, and\\n  check-in/fulfillment history. Calculate recipient pricing independently at\\n  current base prices with recipient-current discounts only, refund each\\n  original Stripe source exactly, and use database-only completion only when\\n  the whole bundle is free and no refund is required.\\n- Google Maps is required production functionality. Cloudflare Images is being\\n  removed and must not gain new product coupling or release-gate requirements.\\n- Dispatch each queued email once. Keep explicit provider rejections and\\n  delivery-unknown outcomes stored and read-only; never requeue or resend an\\n  ambiguous outcome automatically.\\n\\n## Logging\\n\\n- Use Effect platform logging (`Effect.log`, `Effect.logInfo`, `Effect.logWarning`, `Effect.logError`).\\n- Prefer structured log annotations (`Effect.annotateLogs`) over interpolated log strings.\\n- Do not use `consola` or direct `console.*` in server runtime code.\\n- After every server file edit, run `bun run lint` and `bun run format:write`.\\n- Before calling WebStorm `get_file_problems` on edited server files, run `bun run lint` first.\\n- Markdown files do not need a WebStorm `get_file_problems` pass.\\n- After editing a server file, run WebStorm `get_file_problems` on that file when possible before finishing.\\nsrc/db/AGENTS.md\\nsrc/server/effect/AGENTS.md\\n# Database Guidelines\\n\\n- Drizzle schema is the source of truth for persisted shapes.\\n- For Drizzle reference material, inspect the vendored upstream source under `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/**` first.\\n- For relation behavior, query builders, dialect details, and type inference, prefer upstream tests in `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/tests/**`, `repos/drizzle/drizzle-orm/type-tests/**`, and `repos/drizzle/integration-tests/**` over stale examples.\\n- Treat `repos/drizzle` as read-only reference material. Do not import from it; app code should keep importing from normal Drizzle packages.\\n- Prefer inferred Drizzle types across callers; avoid duplicate handwritten DB model types.\\n- Apply local schema changes from the current Drizzle source with\\n  `bun run db:push` or `bun run db:reset`.\\n- Hosted schema changes are explained and allowlisted before the private `ops`\\n  role applies the packaged Drizzle schema. Do not add a parallel repo-local\\n  migration or compatibility path.\\n- Avoid `any`/unchecked casts in query helpers.\\n- Server-side Effect authorization is authoritative; no PostgreSQL RLS layer is\\n  planned.\\n- Composite tenant foreign keys, `NO ACTION` relationships, and database\\n  constraints are integrity safeguards, not authorization mechanisms.\\n- When changing schema or constraints, document required local reset/setup steps in track/handoff docs.\\n- After every DB file edit, run `bun run lint` and `bun run format:write`.\\n- Before calling WebStorm `get_file_problems` on edited DB files, run `bun run lint` first.\\n- Markdown files do not need a WebStorm `get_file_problems` pass.\\n- After editing a DB file, run WebStorm `get_file_problems` on that file when possible before finishing.\\n\\n## Drizzle Relations v2 Schema Design Notes\\n\\n- Define Drizzle relations for query ergonomics, but still define real database foreign keys and constraints in schema tables; relations alone do not create DB constraints.\\n- For many-to-many, model an explicit junction table and prefer a composite primary key on the pair of foreign keys.\\n- If using `through(...)` for relation traversal, index each foreign key column and add a composite index on the pair to support both directions and relation resolution efficiently.\\n- Use relation `alias` when two tables connect in multiple ways (for example `author` and `reviewer`) to avoid ambiguity.\\n- Only mark a relation as non-optional when the linked row is guaranteed to exist; keep nullable/optional relation types accurate.\\n- If relation definitions are split with `defineRelationsPart`, merge parts without overwriting table entries and keep one base part that includes all tables for complete type inference.\\n- Predefined relation `where` filters operate on the target (`to`) table; design polymorphic relation filters with that scope in mind.\\n# Effect library documentation\\n\\nThis documentation resides in the Effect monorepo, which contains the source\\ncode for the Effect library and its related packages.\\n\\nWhen you need to find any information about the Effect library, only use this\\ndocumentation and the source code found in `./packages`. Do not use\\n`node_modules` or any other external documentation, as it may be outdated or\\nincorrect.\\n\\n**Note**: The examples in this documentation contain comments for illustration\\npurposes. In practice, you would not include these comments in your code.\\n\\n## Writing `Effect` code\\n\\nPrefer writing Effect code with `Effect.gen` & `Effect.fn(\\\"name\\\")`. Then attach\\nadditional behaviour with combinators. This style is more readable and easier to\\nmaintain than using combinators alone.\\n\\n### Using Effect.gen\\n\\nUse `Effect.gen` to write code in an imperative style similar to async await.\\nYou can use `yield*` to access the result of an effect.\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { Effect, Schema } from \\\"effect\\\"\\n\\nEffect.gen(function*() {\\n  yield* Effect.log(\\\"Starting the file processing...\\\")\\n  yield* Effect.log(\\\"Reading file...\\\")\\n\\n  // Always return when raising an error, to ensure typescript understands that\\n  // the function will not continue executing.\\n  return yield* new FileProcessingError({ message: \\\"Failed to read the file\\\" })\\n}).pipe(\\n  // Add additional functionality with .pipe\\n  Effect.catch((error) => Effect.logError(`An error occurred: ${error}`)),\\n  Effect.withSpan(\\\"fileProcessing\\\", {\\n    attributes: {\\n      method: \\\"Effect.gen\\\"\\n    }\\n  })\\n)\\n\\n// Use Schema.TaggedErrorClass to define a custom error\\nexport class FileProcessingError extends Schema.TaggedErrorClass<FileProcessingError>()(\\\"FileProcessingError\\\", {\\n  message: Schema.String\\n}) {}\\n```\\n\\n### Using Effect.fn\\n\\nWhen writing functions that return an Effect, use `Effect.fn` to use the\\ngenerator syntax.\\n\\n**Avoid creating functions that return an Effect.gen**, use `Effect.fn`\\ninstead.\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { Effect, Schema } from \\\"effect\\\"\\n\\n// Pass a string to Effect.fn, which will improve stack traces and also\\n// attach a tracing span (using Effect.withSpan behind the scenes).\\n//\\n// The name string should match the function name.\\n//\\nexport const effectFunction = Effect.fn(\\\"effectFunction\\\")(\\n  // You can use `Effect.fn.Return` to specify the return type of the function.\\n  // It accepts the same type parameters as `Effect.Effect`.\\n  function*(n: number): Effect.fn.Return<string, SomeError> {\\n    yield* Effect.logInfo(\\\"Received number:\\\", n)\\n\\n    // Always return when raising an error, to ensure typescript understands that\\n    // the function will not continue executing.\\n    return yield* new SomeError({ message: \\\"Failed to read the file\\\" })\\n  },\\n  // Add additional functionality by passing in additional arguments.\\n  // **Do not** use .pipe with Effect.fn\\n  Effect.catch((error) => Effect.logError(`An error occurred: ${error}`)),\\n  Effect.annotateLogs({\\n    method: \\\"effectFunction\\\"\\n  })\\n)\\n\\n// Use Schema.TaggedErrorClass to define a custom error\\nexport class SomeError extends Schema.TaggedErrorClass<SomeError>()(\\\"SomeError\\\", {\\n  message: Schema.String\\n}) {}\\n```\\n\\n### More examples\\n\\n- **[Creating effects from common sources](./ai-docs/src/01_effect/01_basics/10_creating-effects.ts)**:\\n  Learn how to create effects from various sources, including plain values,\\n  synchronous code, Promise APIs, optional values, and callback-based APIs.\\n\\n## Defining schemas and domain models\\n\\nAll validation and domain modeling in Effect is done with `Schema`.\\n\\n**AVOID using predicates or manual parsing**, instead use `Schema` to parse untrusted data and validate it.\\n\\nFor a comprehensive guide, see [packages/effect/SCHEMA.md](./packages/effect/SCHEMA.md). Make sure to read the guide in chunks, as it is a large document.\\n\\n- **[Schema basics](./ai-docs/src/01_effect/02_schema/10_schema-basics.ts)**:\\n  Define `Schema.Class`s, decode unknown input into typed values, and\\n  encode typed values back into their external representation.\\n\\n## Writing Effect services\\n\\nEffect services are the most common way to structure Effect code. Prefer using\\nservices to encapsulate behaviour over other approaches, as it ensures that your\\ncode is modular, testable, and maintainable.\\n\\n### Context.Service\\n\\nThe default way to define a service is to extend `Context.Service`,\\npassing in the service interface as a type parameter.\\n\\n```ts\\n// file: src/db/Database.ts\\nimport { Context, Effect, Layer, Schema } from \\\"effect\\\"\\n\\n// Pass in the service class name as the first type parameter, and the service\\n// interface as the second type parameter.\\nexport class Database extends Context.Service<Database, {\\n  query(sql: string): Effect.Effect<Array<unknown>, DatabaseError>\\n}>()(\\n  // The string identifier for the service, which should include the package\\n  // name and the subdirectory path to the service file.\\n  \\\"myapp/db/Database\\\"\\n) {\\n  // Attach a static layer to the service, which will be used to provide an\\n  // implementation of the service.\\n  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(\\n    Database,\\n    Effect.gen(function*() {\\n      // Define the service methods using Effect.fn\\n      const query = Effect.fn(\\\"Database.query\\\")(function*(sql: string) {\\n        yield* Effect.log(\\\"Executing SQL query:\\\", sql)\\n        return [{ id: 1, name: \\\"Alice\\\" }, { id: 2, name: \\\"Bob\\\" }]\\n      })\\n\\n      // Return an instance of the service using Database.of, passing in an\\n      // object that implements the service interface.\\n      return Database.of({\\n        query\\n      })\\n    })\\n  )\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a06cdc-6399-7b30-9fa8-4b87f0cfba0e","create_time":1788571235.552607}}}
