cs), all 1,698 server tests, 1,028 app tests, 174 PostgreSQL tests and both infrastructure roots pass. Fixture/transaction readback is empty. The canonical image passes after correcting checkout permissions without changing source bytes or relaxing scan flags: 95,778,395 bytes and zero HIGH/CRITICAL vulnerabilities across 296 targets including unfixed entries. Root independently verified all 45 before/after blobs and 11 raw logs. The final local draft is entry 17 in the manifest; final Browser/docs/provider acceptance remains separate.\n\nCanonical checks explicitly use Node 24.15.0 from NVM and Bun 1.3.14. The Homebrew node@24 alias resolves to a different major version and is not accepted as runtime evidence. Image scans include unfixed HIGH/CRITICAL findings; database update checks remain enabled. [Earlier provenance supplement](layer00-04-node-provenance.md).\n\n## Current application checkpoint\n\nThe assembly is clean at `16f2a0a36834569e19355592b44499de0bf129a1` (tree `16c2af7a155b65bb7acf3984cbae8f68cac85ebb`). Its two-file fixture/queue correction preserves all three scenarios and32 assertions while observing rejection immediately, retaining rollback and worker failures, discarding a client after failed rollback and attempting every cleanup. Fresh strict checking (1 root/122 sources/0 diagnostics), all1,674 server,1,342 app and123 PostgreSQL tests pass. Targeted URL/concurrency/announcement tenant and transaction counters are zero. [Checkpoint evidence](assembly-discovery-fixture-checkpoint.json) binds the final code to the passing executions and independent review. Production source remains identical to56e7; final runtime and Browser acceptance remain pending.\n\nThe prior assembly checkpoint is `56e7fdc90802f8478b2d56eb6de88bf1db01bac7` (tree `811989533a546642300de116e91822d053cd4576`). Its 14-file profile, cache, row-removal and truthful-copy checkpoint passes all1,674 server tests,1,342 app tests and123 PostgreSQL tests, with zero diagnostics across8 strict roots and104 first-party sources. Lint, format, release-note and diff checks pass; fixture/transaction readback is empty. [Checkpoint evidence](assembly-profile-complete-checkpoint.json) binds final code bytes to the passing source and PostgreSQL snapshots. The failed lint, stale permission fixture and disabled-button fixture assumption are retained with their corrections. F228 profile/card lifetime and home/offline copy, F231 stable row tracking and F232 current-value cache updates are complete at this source checkpoint. Fresh complete assembly runtime, Browser and documentation publication remain pending. No app runtime or Pages preview is running.\n\nThe prior authoring checkpoint `8ca0b13bd03186a2689898d35bc4525e6024d4ad` retains its25-file source/PG proof in [its receipt](assembly-authoring-complete-checkpoint.json): all nine authoring components, sibling-read lifetime fixes and F230 retained event paging, with1,674server/1,308app/123PG tests passing.\n\nThe preceding clean assembly head `948dc033c508c9249d3e7f0096a43e2c856b1d12` (tree `b50e8562db84df2b18b25cdb809d66edfd293654`) records the 16-path feedback/fixture/documentation batch. All 1,674 server tests, 1,185 app tests and 123 PostgreSQL tests pass; the 12-root/191-source strict graph has zero diagnostics. Final lint, format and diff checks pass. The source-equivalent committed bytes and retained failures are bound in [checkpoint evidence](assembly-feedback-checkpoint.json). Its new runtime/publication acceptance remains pending. The prior app runtime and Pages preview are stopped; all exact-project app containers, networks and volumes are removed. The previous generated Pages catalog is preserved in a verified212-file archive, and only its owned generated roots were restored to the clean upstream consumer for regeneration.\n\nClean test head `4dd644f784ccb534effb838656634f083d282206` now passes all 167 functional tests, 51 documentation tests, 11 Auth0/Google Maps integration tests and nine live ESNcard release tests plus nine provider-error unit cases, with no retries or incomplete tests. It corrects one stale add-on transfer-message assertion; the prior 166/167 failure remains recorded. The runtime was built from `c5f8a1b10a6e03a896dad7694c57c851c61f6d4c`; the one-file comparison proves that application, server, schema, dependencies and build inputs are unchanged. The [runtime receipt](assembly-4dd644-runtime-gates.json) records both identities and all log hashes. The complete combined documentation publication also passes54/54 and installs13 guides into an isolated Pages checkout. Its 19 tooling tests, lint, diff check and production build pass under the separate pinned Pages runtime (Node 24.11.0/Bun 1.3.14). The installed catalog contains 13 guides and 199 PNGs with 203 references, no missing or unused images, and no retired output. Search and guide navigation work in the production preview, and the neutral email crop passes visual review. Final visual acceptance remains held: F-229 records a stale prior approval notification in the missing-file receipt screenshot. A separate companion anchor-offset issue is under review. The exact installed output has a verified local archive. That prior owned app runtime has now been stopped and its exact project resources removed.\n\nThe preceding implementation checkpoint is `c5f8a1b10a6e03a896dad7694c57c851c61f6d4c` (33 changed paths). Payment review copy, normalized copied transfer codes, locked check-in timing, transferred add-on reads, copied paid-template checks and fixture ownership repairs pass 1,674 server tests, 1,172 app tests and 123 PostgreSQL tests. The final 24-root strict graph has zero diagnostics. Exact source hashes retain the earlier app/PG results across the last test-only additions, and database readback confirms no owned RLS objects or fixture tenants remain. [Clean source receipt](assembly-ticket-fixture-final-source.json). Its production runtime is the verified source-equivalent runtime for the complete 4dd644 acceptance above.\n\nThe previous complete runtime checkpoint is the clean assembly head `5d49f011c4d6f467c3d74d0241b99173f5a6f87f`. It adds audit-specific microsecond cursors, readable audit descriptions, effective role details and a durable wildcard rename/revocation journey. Full server tests pass 214 files / 1,671 tests, app tests 145 / 1,148, and PostgreSQL tests 20 / 115. The 11-root strict graph has zero diagnostics, and audit fixture readback is empty. [Current receipt](assembly-5d49-verification-evidence.json).\n\nManual Browser acceptance at this head confirms that role details display effective wildcard grants, a name-only save preserves both stored wildcards, and explicitly revoking Manage payments preserves every other grant after reload. The temporary role was unassigned throughout and exact cleanup readback is zero. [Browser evidence](assembly-5d49-role-browser-evidence.json). Full functional acceptance passes 165 / 165, documentation 51 / 51, Auth0/Google Maps integration 11 / 11, and live ESNcard certification 9 / 9 plus all nine provider-error unit tests. There are no retries or incomplete tests. The owned server was gracefully stopped afterward, and all containers in its exact project are confirmed removed. [Complete runtime gate receipt](assembly-5d49-runtime-gates.json).\n\nThe later clean `30ab75c416569dbf3854ab839b08757f242e31ea` checkpoint adds participant input limits, template description feedback and cancellation of stalled email response bodies. All 214 server files / 1,672 tests and 145 app files / 1,152 tests pass. The strict check covers six edited roots and 225 sources with zero diagnostics, using the project's actual test ambient types. A rebuilt production runtime passes all ten collected registration/add-on Browser tests (seven setup tests and three journeys), including repeated guest clamping, the exact answer boundary and the original successful signup/readback. Its owned containers, network and volumes are removed. [Checkpoint receipt](assembly-input-email-checkpoint.json). These focused results do not replace the full final-stack runtime acceptance.\n\nThe previous clean `6b4b263b` checkpoint passed 164 functional tests, 51 documentation tests, 11 Auth0/Google Maps integration tests, nine live ESNcard tests and nine provider-error unit cases, all without retries. [Previous complete runtime receipt](assembly-6b4b-verification-evidence.json). Those results are historical evidence and do not certify the later head.\n\nThe fixture cleanup work preserves 1,549 assertions, 95 test titles and 247 screenshot/documentation calls while registering cleanup before writes and making scenario setup transactional. [Preservation evidence](assembly-f212-preservation.json). Route teardown, reporter redaction and shell argument-forwarding repairs retain their focused regression receipts. Earlier failed runs remain available with their source and cause; sensitive diagnostics have owner-only permissions.\n\n## Material findings and repairs\n\n- F-230: a failed later event-list page hid already loaded links and made recovery unreachable. The two-file correction and real infinite-query failure/retry regression pass the complete assembly source suite at8ca0b13. Fresh runtime and Browser acceptance remain pending.\n- F-228 profile follow-up: operation lifetime, retained input and confirmed-save/read-failure recovery are integrated at56e7fdc9, including the separate Unauthorized/home-action copy companions. Full source and PostgreSQL checks pass; fresh runtime acceptance remains pending. The original sealed packets remain unchanged.\n\n- F-216: role forms could discard wildcard authority during a name-only edit; details also omitted effective grants. Both are repaired, with rendered tests and the complete manual save/revoke/reload journey. The durable new Playwright case passes in the full 165-test functional run.\n- F-217/F-218: audit cursors lost database timestamp precision, and description summaries could display encoded text. The actual PostgreSQL pagination regression and complete server/app suites pass after repair.\n- Discount snapshots could pass a PostgreSQL CHECK with a NULL applied price. The repair has an old-constraint failure witness and real database coverage. Existing incomplete rows block the new constraint; financial history is not reconstructed. [Receipt](assembly-8e06ea-snapshot-evidence.json).\n- Payment checkout binding, receipt promotion ownership, expired transfer offers, event discount snapshots, onboarding navigation, conditional ESNcard updates and receipt UX have implemented repairs. Their final stack layers and integrated journeys still need complete acceptance; see the repository [review queue](../evorto/APPLICATION_REVIEW_QUEUE.md).\n- One full server run exceeded a five-second shell-fixture test deadline under contention. An independently checked, suite-local 15-second budget preserves production process limits and every command assertion. The corrected full suite passes; the failed run is retained in the current receipt.\n\n## Newly confirmed client follow-ups\n\n- F-231: actual first-row removal in EventEdit throws NG01904 because row tracking reads an orphaned Signal Forms field. The minimal identity-tracking fix and three public deletion/save regressions pass all26 EventEdit cases in the layer15 donor suite. The two-file assembly companion is integrated at56e7fdc9; complete source and PostgreSQL checks pass, with fresh runtime pending.\n- F-232: the home-organization action writes prefix-only cache keys, leaving the real self/maybeSelf entries stale. Once those keys are corrected, the home/profile success callbacks can also overwrite newer unrelated fields from captured user snapshots. The correction at56e7fdc9 updates only each action's owned fields in the current matching-user cache; four real component cases include both overlapping completion orders and preservation of a different-user failed/invalidated cache. Full source/PG pass; fresh runtime remains pending.\n- F-228: home-action unknown/Unauthorized messages and the event-conflict latest-details claim have narrow corrections at56e7fdc9, passing the complete app suite. The original mutation and permission rules stay in place.\n\n## Product decisions awaiting the user\n\n- D-009: product docs specify global user-owned ESNcards, while persistence is tenant-scoped.\n- D-010: platform principals lack dedicated audited operations for 19 ordinary settings fields, onboarding and branding.\n- D-011: multiple real tenant origins require an explicit Auth0 callback/cookie, DNS/TLS, runtime-domain and storage-CORS design.\n- D-012: uncertain payment incidents need an audited operator recovery workflow or an explicit release blocker.\n\nThese questions were already raised. No product answer or permissive role workaround is assumed.\n\n## Remaining work\n\n1. The complete nine-component authoring batch is committed with passing source/PostgreSQL checks. The profile/card, current-cache, row-removal and truthful-copy follow-ups are committed with full source/PG checks; rebuild the exact assembly, run complete runtime acceptance, regenerate the Pages catalog and repeat build/visual acceptance. Preserve earlier checkpoints as historical evidence.\n2. Materialize the remaining concerns, measure each actual diff below 100 paths, and run every triggered local CI suite at each final head.\n3. Complete branch-authentic acceptance, refresh remote state, publish the verified draft stack with gh-stack, and place the original PRs while retaining backups.\n\nThe disposable PostgreSQL state file contains credentials and must not be copied into review artifacts. Only explicitly owned local runtime resources are used; unrelated Docker stacks remain untouched.\n\n## Latest read-only remote refresh\n\nAt 2026-09-04 23:21:54 UTC, GitHub main remains `d117229d3d7d357af9f9fc61fdad8d348d6809ea`; PR123 and PR124 remain open on main at their original preserved he