e ESNcard tests and nine provider-error unit cases, all without retries. [Previous complete runtime receipt](assembly-6b4b-verification-evidence.json). Those results are historical evidence and do not certify the later head.\n\nThe fixture cleanup work preserves 1,549 assertions, 95 test titles and 247 screenshot/documentation calls while registering cleanup before writes and making scenario setup transactional. [Preservation evidence](assembly-f212-preservation.json). Route teardown, reporter redaction and shell argument-forwarding repairs retain their focused regression receipts. Earlier failed runs remain available with their source and cause; sensitive diagnostics have owner-only permissions.\n\n## Material findings and repairs\n\n- F-230: a failed later event-list page hid already loaded links and made recovery unreachable. The two-file correction and real infinite-query failure/retry regression pass the complete assembly source suite at8ca0b13. Fresh runtime and Browser acceptance remain pending.\n- F-228 profile follow-up: operation lifetime, retained input and confirmed-save/read-failure recovery are integrated at56e7fdc9, including the separate Unauthorized/home-action copy companions. Full source and PostgreSQL checks pass; fresh runtime acceptance remains pending. The original sealed packets remain unchanged.\n\n- F-216: role forms could discard wildcard authority during a name-only edit; details also omitted effective grants. Both are repaired, with rendered tests and the complete manual save/revoke/reload journey. The durable new Playwright case passes in the full 165-test functional run.\n- F-217/F-218: audit cursors lost database timestamp precision, and description summaries could display encoded text. The actual PostgreSQL pagination regression and complete server/app suites pass after repair.\n- Discount snapshots could pass a PostgreSQL CHECK with a NULL applied price. The repair has an old-constraint failure witness and real database coverage. Existing incomplete rows block the new constraint; financial history is not reconstructed. [Receipt](assembly-8e06ea-snapshot-evidence.json).\n- Payment checkout binding, receipt promotion ownership, expired transfer offers, event discount snapshots, onboarding navigation, conditional ESNcard updates and receipt UX have implemented repairs. Their final stack layers and integrated journeys still need complete acceptance; see the repository [review queue](../evorto/APPLICATION_REVIEW_QUEUE.md).\n- One full server run exceeded a five-second shell-fixture test deadline under contention. An independently checked, suite-local 15-second budget preserves production process limits and every command assertion. The corrected full suite passes; the failed run is retained in the current receipt.\n\n## Newly confirmed client follow-ups\n\n- F-231: actual first-row removal in EventEdit throws NG01904 because row tracking reads an orphaned Signal Forms field. The minimal identity-tracking fix and three public deletion/save regressions pass all26 EventEdit cases in the layer15 donor suite. The two-file assembly companion is integrated at56e7fdc9; complete source and PostgreSQL checks pass, with fresh runtime pending.\n- F-232: the home-organization action writes prefix-only cache keys, leaving the real self/maybeSelf entries stale. Once those keys are corrected, the home/profile success callbacks can also overwrite newer unrelated fields from captured user snapshots. The correction at56e7fdc9 updates only each action's owned fields in the current matching-user cache; four real component cases include both overlapping completion orders and preservation of a different-user failed/invalidated cache. Full source/PG pass; fresh runtime remains pending.\n- F-228: home-action unknown/Unauthorized messages and the event-conflict latest-details claim have narrow corrections at56e7fdc9, passing the complete app suite. The original mutation and permission rules stay in place.\n\n## Product decisions awaiting the user\n\n- D-009: product docs specify global user-owned ESNcards, while persistence is tenant-scoped.\n- D-010: platform principals lack dedicated audited operations for 19 ordinary settings fields, onboarding and branding.\n- D-011: multiple real tenant origins require an explicit Auth0 callback/cookie, DNS/TLS, runtime-domain and storage-CORS design.\n- D-012: uncertain payment incidents need an audited operator recovery workflow or an explicit release blocker.\n\nThese questions were already raised. No product answer or permissive role workaround is assumed.\n\n## Remaining work\n\n1. The complete nine-component authoring batch is committed with passing source/PostgreSQL checks. The profile/card, current-cache, row-removal and truthful-copy follow-ups are committed with full source/PG checks; rebuild the exact assembly, run complete runtime acceptance, regenerate the Pages catalog and repeat build/visual acceptance. Preserve earlier checkpoints as historical evidence.\n2. Materialize the remaining concerns, measure each actual diff below 100 paths, and run every triggered local CI suite at each final head.\n3. Complete branch-authentic acceptance, refresh remote state, publish the verified draft stack with gh-stack, and place the original PRs while retaining backups.\n\nThe disposable PostgreSQL state file contains credentials and must not be copied into review artifacts. Only explicitly owned local runtime resources are used; unrelated Docker stacks remain untouched.\n\n## Latest read-only remote refresh\n\nAt 2026-09-04 23:21:54 UTC, GitHub main remains `d117229d3d7d357af9f9fc61fdad8d348d6809ea`; PR123 and PR124 remain open on main at their original preserved heads. Both local backup refs still match. [Receipt](remote-check-20260904232154.json). No remote writes occurred.\n"},"started_at_ms":1788573235432,"completed_at_ms":1788573235432}}
